WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.964 --> 00:00:05.630
JORDAN TIGANI: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:07.270
My name is Jordan Tigani.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.910
I'm a software
engineer at BigQuery.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.815
I was one of the original
BigQuery engineers.

00:00:11.815 --> 00:00:13.600
I've been working on
it for a long time,

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.070
wrote a book about it.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:16.690
I'm managing part
of the team now,

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:18.820
but it hasn't been
so long that I've

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:21.770
been managing that I have
forgotten how everything works.

00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:26.930
A couple of prerequisites.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.400
This is a highly technical talk.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.250
If you're not here to
hear technical talk,

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:36.400
then it might not be for you.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:41.860
I'm not going to give
any pitches, or sales,

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:42.654
or marketing stuff.

00:00:42.654 --> 00:00:44.320
I'm just going to
talk about technology.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:50.500
The old only pitch
you'll hear will

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:53.000
be I'm going to try to convince
you that we have some pretty

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.180
cool technology in BigQuery.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.850
So the problem that I'm trying
to solve with this talk is

00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:01.330
really to solve this
anti-pattern that we see a lot,

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:02.070
which is--

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.360
we get a customer, they
start using BigQuery,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:05.550
everything's working great.

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:09.340
Their queries are super
fast, they're happy,

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.691
and then they find that
they hit a roadblock.

00:01:11.691 --> 00:01:13.190
They hit something
that runs slowly.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.284
It doesn't run at all.

00:01:15.284 --> 00:01:16.700
And they don't
know how to fix it.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.790
And because the
BigQuery architecture

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:20.800
is so different than
what they're used to,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.420
they don't have
a lot of options.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.690
So they either post on Stack
Overflow, they email support.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:31.020
Or now that we're
selling to enterprises,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.180
a lot of enterprise people have
the phone numbers of Google VPs

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.200
and so we get nasty
emails that have

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:39.940
to be solved really quickly.

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:41.440
Can anybody guess
which one of these

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.320
is most effective in getting
their problem solved?

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.840
Yes, Stack Overflow is
usually going to work.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.630
Actually, no, our support
folks are really good, as well.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.312
But it's nice to have a--

00:01:55.312 --> 00:01:56.770
Stack Overflow, we
have a community

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:04.360
of people that are quite helpful
and often they work for free

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.180
and are very helpful.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.680
So I'm going to walk through
some BigQuery query plans

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.160
and show you how those query
plans map to the architecture.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.770
And I'm going to go
into the architecture

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:24.310
and try to give you an
idea of a mental model

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.920
so you can develop an intuition
about what's going to work,

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:30.220
what's not going to work.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:33.590
This talk is called
Advanced BigQuery.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.840
I'm not going to show a whole
lot of advanced BigQuery

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:36.339
features.

00:02:36.339 --> 00:02:39.830
I'm just going to try to
show how an advanced BigQuery

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:43.100
user would use BigQuery.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.500
To start out I'm going
to review, a little bit,

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:49.130
the architecture of Dremel,
which is the query engine

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:50.964
that BigQuery uses.

00:02:50.964 --> 00:02:52.880
And this picture might
look a little different

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.380
if you've read the Dremel paper.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.380
Things have changed in
the last couple of years.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.420
It's no longer a
static tree, but we

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.880
have a dynamic tree that
can sort of assemble

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.660
different stages as needed.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.040
And I'll show a lot more
of that in a minute.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.080
But as always, we read
from columnar store--

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.170
super fast, colossus data store.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.430
Highly distributed.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:22.020
That's one of the
keys to our success.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.950
So let's go through a
simple query execution.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.410
Do a filter and an
account aggregation.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.750
So how does this work?

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.180
Stage one, we can
apply the filter.

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:35.360
We can do partial
counts in parallel.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:37.550
Stage two, then we
can compute the sum

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.102
of those partial counts.

00:03:39.102 --> 00:03:41.060
You'll see there's some
slot numbers over here.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.010
I'll describe what
those mean and where

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.910
we get them in a second.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:47.580
So this is what the query plan
looks like for that query.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.360
And you can find this
in the BigQuery UI.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:52.610
I'll show you in a
couple of minutes.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.927
You can see the query
plan from your queries,

00:03:58.927 --> 00:04:00.510
but there's a lot
of information here.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.810
So I'm going to go
over it relatively

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:07.250
slowly so it makes it clear.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.740
So here we have, basically,
which part of the query

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:12.140
is running at each stage.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.450
They may look a little
different than the query texts

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.033
that you wrote because
this is what it

00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:18.589
looks like to our query engine.

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:21.829
So here we're reading
from the underlying table

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:24.560
and we're applying the filter.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:27.140
And actually, for
this kind of filter,

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:31.400
we can actually push that
into the storage layer

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.660
to get a much, much
faster query performance.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:37.760
But then we're also
doing the aggregation

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.650
of the COUNT_STAR at this
level and writing that

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:44.670
to the next stage.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:49.040
So the next stage, we read
from the previous stage output.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:51.680
We compute the sum of
the partial counts,

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.910
and we return the total sum.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.000
Then there's these four
timing windows here.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.590
The first one is Wait.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:05.780
And the wait time in this
one is not particularly large

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:08.210
because we didn't actually
have to wait for anything.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.000
One thing to note is
that this only starts

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:13.490
when that stage starts running.

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:17.210
So you can see in the
second stage, the stage

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:19.190
two started running
at the same time

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:21.890
stage one did because as soon
as it gets partial output,

00:05:21.890 --> 00:05:24.920
it can start
aggregating the results.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.090
In general, these have
three different colors

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:29.930
or three different gradations.

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:32.570
There's kind of the
faintest one, which means

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:34.770
it didn't really do anything.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:38.854
There's the darkest one,
which is the average time.

00:05:38.854 --> 00:05:40.520
And then there's a
little less dark one,

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:42.410
which is the max time.

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:44.545
So since we're running
these things in parallel,

00:05:44.545 --> 00:05:45.920
we have lots of
parallel workers.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.674
This is sort of how we
represent the parallelism.

00:05:50.674 --> 00:05:52.090
In this case, Read
didn't do much.

00:05:52.090 --> 00:05:53.930
This is reading from
I/O or this was not

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.270
a significant bottleneck.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.640
The Compute stage is a
little more interesting here.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.650
The compute stage--
the average time

00:06:03.650 --> 00:06:06.020
is actually where we
spend most of the time.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.410
And the max time
wasn't much higher.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.937
So this is actually a
pretty healthy query

00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:15.020
or a pretty healthy pattern
because we didn't have

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.470
a lot of long tail effects.

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:19.800
And Writes was fairly minimal.

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:25.910
OK, so the next bit of
information that we have

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:28.940
is the Input and Output sizes.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:33.440
In this query, we read
over a billion rows.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.570
And we output 220 rows.

00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:38.210
Can anybody guess what
this 220 number means?

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:44.770
So this is the
available parallelism.

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:47.520
This is actually how
many parallel machines

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.470
we're running because
each one of these things

00:06:49.470 --> 00:06:53.580
is doing a parallel count
and each one outputs one row.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:59.130
So we can kind of see, by
using these query plans,

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:04.470
how many resources BigQuery
is throwing at the problem.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:08.820
And because the wait time
is low, none of these

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:12.066
had to wait to be scheduled.

00:07:12.066 --> 00:07:13.440
We can actually
know that this is

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.760
how many resources were actually
used during the inquiry.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:25.620
The next stage then
reads those 220 rows

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:29.330
and outputs just the single one.

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:32.270
So a lot of data
just on one slide.

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:34.775
Hopefully it will make the
next ones go more smoothly.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:41.960
If we increase the
size of the tables

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:45.036
that we're querying
over, we can look at what

00:07:45.036 --> 00:07:46.160
happened to the query plan.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.290
So this is the query
plan for the same query

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:51.540
but just running over a data
set that's 10 times as large.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.360
So we can see this
is 10 billion rows.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:55.970
It outputs 2000 rows.

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:59.690
And then one
interesting difference

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:04.070
here is, while the compute
time is still very high,

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.860
the wait time has
gone from almost zero

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:08.420
to something much higher.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:12.500
And what this means is that
the available power parallelism

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.730
was higher than the
scheduleable parallelism, which

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:19.670
means that we had to wait
before we could actually

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:21.420
schedule some of these.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:26.150
So ways you can avoid that
would be buy more slots.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:30.860
You could run over
smaller data sets.

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:34.039
Or have queries that--

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:37.830
the compute stages that
execute more quickly.

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:41.929
So if we increase by
another order of magnitude

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:45.890
to a 100 billion
row data set, we

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:48.530
can see that actually the
compute time has gone down

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.500
to a very small amount.

00:08:50.500 --> 00:08:52.790
And almost the whole time
are waiting for slots

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:56.800
because we can see here
that we have 21,000 slots.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.850
Or 21,000 is our
available parallelism.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.730
And these numbers get a little
bit important in a second.

00:09:04.730 --> 00:09:07.580
So it's the same
query execution,

00:09:07.580 --> 00:09:10.970
it just means that we
have a lot more slots.

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.970
And we have the introduction
of this scheduler process.

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.760
And this scheduler
is what actually

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.350
schedules different
pieces of execution

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.980
to run on the different charts.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:25.220
OK, so some background
on BigQuery slots.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:29.000
We get a lot of
questions on slots--

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.690
what they mean, how to use
them, how many you need.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:35.240
So the slot is a single
unit of scheduling.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:39.260
It's hard to say what
it actually means,

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:40.430
in terms of resources.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:43.671
But it is some sort
of physical resources.

00:09:43.671 --> 00:09:45.670
Not all slots have the
same amount of resources.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:49.940
So some can have more RAM, some
can have more CPU, more I/O.

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:53.870
But you can sort of think of
it as one scheduleable unit

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.600
of parallel execution.

00:09:56.600 --> 00:09:59.030
These can be restarted
by the scheduler.

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:00.375
They can be canceled.

00:10:00.375 --> 00:10:02.770
We'll show some
queries in a little bit

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:06.315
that can be canceled.

00:10:06.315 --> 00:10:09.930
The other key thing is that it's
not a measure of performance.

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.952
So some people say,
well, I want more slots.

00:10:11.952 --> 00:10:13.410
How do you get more
slots because I

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:14.940
want my queries run faster.

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:15.810
Well, your queries
are only going

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:17.190
to run faster if
you're exhausting

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:18.310
the available parallelism.

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:20.310
So you're just getting
more throughput.

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:24.840
Often that translates into
faster queries but not always.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.710
So by default, everybody
gets 2000 slots.

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:33.510
That's the amount of
parallel things you can run.

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:38.490
Our scheduler balances
fairly between queries

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:41.430
within a project and then
between projects, overall,

00:10:41.430 --> 00:10:45.300
because this is a shared
multi-tenent system.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.830
Slots can be
scheduled individually

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.900
for each stage of a query.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:55.792
And I'm going to
show a slot demo,

00:10:55.792 --> 00:10:57.000
if I can wake my computer up.

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:11.990
All right, so this is
the Wikipedia benchmark.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:14.050
This is a one
billion row data set.

00:11:14.050 --> 00:11:18.634
And running this query should
take about six seconds.

00:11:18.634 --> 00:11:20.050
Let's see how long
it takes today.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:21.580
Six and 1/2 seconds.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.390
And we can see that
it had 220 slots.

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:29.960
This is, essentially, the same
thing that we saw earlier.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:33.580
Here's the 100
billion row query.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:36.370
And this takes about
20 seconds to run

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.520
when we don't do anything else.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:41.780
And let's try running
the one billion

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:45.800
row query at the same time
we're running the 100 billion

00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:46.810
or query.

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:51.214
So what should happen
is the one billion

00:11:51.214 --> 00:11:53.380
row query should still get
220 slots because we have

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:57.760
a fair scheduler that says, if
we have two queries running,

00:11:57.760 --> 00:11:59.770
each one gets to
use half the slots.

00:11:59.770 --> 00:12:03.850
So this query should have run
about as fast as it did before.

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:06.460
And the other query should run,
OK, maybe a little bit slower

00:12:06.460 --> 00:12:07.010
than before.

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:08.176
So this a little bit slower.

00:12:08.176 --> 00:12:10.360
It's about 20 seconds.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:14.740
Now let's try running 200
billion row slot, 100 billion

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:16.860
row queries at the same time.

00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:21.640
And then we can throw in our
little, small one billion

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.790
row query, as well.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:27.140
Small, one billion.

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:30.490
So if we think of what should
happen when we do this,

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.490
the one billion row query
should still get 220 slots.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:37.520
And then the two larger ones
should split the difference.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:39.940
So they should each
get about 900 slots

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:44.170
and they should each take about
50% longer or about 30 seconds.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:45.790
OK, this one took 25 seconds.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:46.970
That took 28 seconds.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:50.020
So we can kind of see
how the slot scheduler

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.710
works in these query times.

00:12:53.710 --> 00:12:56.602
And it generally
works pretty well.

00:12:56.602 --> 00:13:01.390
You can see we spent a
lot of time in wait time.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:04.480
Can we switch back to
the presentation, please?

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:14.220
Can we switch back?

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:16.510
There we go.

00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:20.330
OK, so that was sort of
really simple queries.

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:23.990
What if we want to do some
more interesting things?

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:26.690
And what can we know about
the BigQuery architecture

00:13:26.690 --> 00:13:29.180
from other queries?

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:34.970
So one problem that at least
initial versions of Dremel had

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:37.010
were Aggregation with
High Cardinality.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.670
So let's say you wanted to
do an aggregation where--

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:45.410
in this case, if there's lots
of distinct values of foo.

00:13:45.410 --> 00:13:52.370
So what would happen is we would
compute these partial counts

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:53.760
in parallel.

00:13:53.760 --> 00:13:59.320
And then we would push those
back up to the next stage.

00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:03.800
But one problem is that we can't
actually remove any information

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:07.080
until we've seen all of it.

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:12.050
So this master node has
to see, in this case,

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:22.110
that even though they only
get five from Z that they have

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:25.380
to keep that around until
all the remaining nodes

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:27.220
have completed.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.760
And then only finally
after the lower level

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.400
nodes have completed can
they apply the limit.

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:39.120
This would often overwhelm
this master node.

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.260
So we don't do that anymore.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:44.010
We use shuffle in the middle.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:45.730
Or in general, we
don't use that anymore.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:48.510
So if we have the
same input data,

00:14:48.510 --> 00:14:52.170
we're going to apply another
step which does a shuffle.

00:14:52.170 --> 00:14:54.010
And so what shuffle
does is it says,

00:14:54.010 --> 00:14:56.160
OK, we're going to put
all of the things that

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:59.130
hash into this bucket
over here and put

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.350
all the things that hash
into this bucket over here.

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:04.290
And because we have all
of the As on the one

00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:06.180
side and all of the
Zs on the other side,

00:15:06.180 --> 00:15:14.070
we can sort of make logical
decisions about those.

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:17.490
We can apply the
limit at this node

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:20.940
before we then send it
up to the master node.

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:24.794
So this makes it scale
a lot more nicely.

00:15:24.794 --> 00:15:26.460
So how does this look
in the query plan?

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:31.080
So here's an example
using the same data set.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.150
And a couple of
things to call out.

00:15:33.150 --> 00:15:34.530
So you can see the shuffle here.

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:36.970
You can see that when
we do this aggregation,

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.740
we write to a shuffle by hash.

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:41.610
So that's going to hash
these into a number

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:43.560
of different buckets.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:47.640
And then we can also see that
this number is now much higher.

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:52.650
It's no longer the source
number of slots that we used.

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:55.170
This is the number
of shuffle sinks.

00:15:55.170 --> 00:15:57.540
So this is the number
of how many places

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:03.680
we wrote the shuffle data to.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:07.850
So just to go back
and reiterate this.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:11.810
We can apply the partial
group by at the first level.

00:16:11.810 --> 00:16:15.650
We do this magic shuffle.

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:18.050
We can do the
remainder of the group

00:16:18.050 --> 00:16:21.390
by, sort it, and apply a limit.

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:25.990
And finally, the final
sort and final limit.

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:29.610
Sort of, the merge sort and
end limit at the master node.

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:34.522
And this slide just
shows that we can have

00:16:34.522 --> 00:16:35.730
any number of these shuffles.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:42.110
In the inner query, we're
grouping by language and title.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:44.110
The outer query we're
just grouping by language.

00:16:44.110 --> 00:16:46.260
So we need to do two shuffles.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:49.510
And that is
generally no problem.

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:53.560
So whenever stage
end doesn't know

00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:56.860
how many outputs
it's going to have,

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:59.540
we generally shuffle in between.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:01.360
This is the one
thing in the talk

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:03.760
that I can't talk
about how it works.

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:05.800
But it is very fast.

00:17:05.800 --> 00:17:07.849
It's very magic.

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:11.560
It is an in-memory shuffle.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:14.141
Which also means that there's
a limited amount of memory.

00:17:14.141 --> 00:17:15.849
If you use too much--
just like if you're

00:17:15.849 --> 00:17:17.770
using too much on
your local machine--

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:20.251
you start paging
and then performance

00:17:20.251 --> 00:17:21.250
starts getting terrible.

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:22.579
So often we get--

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:24.579
every once in a while we
get reports of somebody

00:17:24.579 --> 00:17:26.140
who's doing a giant query.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:29.230
It exhausts their
shuffle limit and, all

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:31.510
of a sudden, their
performance goes

00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:36.900
from being very fast
to much, much lower.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:39.620
And then the shuffle quotas.

00:17:39.620 --> 00:17:44.030
We have a default shuffle quota,
I think, of two terabytes.

00:17:44.030 --> 00:17:49.130
And that's sort of the actual
in-memory compress data.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.687
But that scales up for the
number of reserve slots

00:17:51.687 --> 00:17:52.270
that you have.

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:57.200
OK, so aggregations are simple.

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:59.390
This is all relatively
straightforward.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:01.340
What if you want to do a join?

00:18:01.340 --> 00:18:03.920
So when you're joining
to large data sets,

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:05.600
we applied the same trick.

00:18:08.630 --> 00:18:12.710
We use hash and shuffle
to shuffle the left table.

00:18:12.710 --> 00:18:14.150
And we shuffle
the right table so

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:17.090
that the matching keys
end up at the same spot

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:21.520
and we can do a local join.

00:18:21.520 --> 00:18:25.480
In the query plan, this looks
like this looks like this.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:30.120
You can see that we're reading
from each side of the join.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:35.880
We are writing it out
to the stage output

00:18:35.880 --> 00:18:37.920
and shuffling by hash.

00:18:37.920 --> 00:18:44.490
And then here we can
see the join keywords

00:18:44.490 --> 00:18:46.837
are "join each with each."

00:18:46.837 --> 00:18:49.170
Now it used to be that if you
wanted to do a large join,

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:53.190
you would have to add the
"each" keyword, which was sort

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:54.840
of nonstandard and not great.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:19:00.230
Now we'll figure out if we
need to do an "each" join.

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:03.460
The alternative--
OK and also see

00:19:03.460 --> 00:19:08.540
we had to shuffle a
large amount of data.

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:12.050
But in stage three, we didn't
have to read all the rows.

00:19:12.050 --> 00:19:13.520
Because we were
doing a limit, we

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:17.810
were able to cancel
the rest of the shards

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:21.600
because we already
had read enough data

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:24.310
and only had to
read a million rows.

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:27.840
So if you're doing
a small join--

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:32.110
if you're joining a large
table with a smaller table,

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:34.830
you don't need to do this
shuffle because that would

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:37.050
be really expensive
to have to move

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.365
all of the data in both
tables to each location.

00:19:40.365 --> 00:19:44.550
So what you can do is basically
called a broadcast join where

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.980
the small table just
gets, essentially,

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:52.320
sent to every single node that's
processing the larger table.

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:55.905
And then we can see
this in the query plan.

00:19:59.430 --> 00:20:05.460
So first we shuffled the
right side of the table

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.020
but then we coalesced it down.

00:20:07.020 --> 00:20:10.260
Because maybe we were
reading 10 different shards.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:15.440
We wanted to get it
into a single shard.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:19.200
We shuffled that down
in this pad coalesce

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.480
and then we did an
"each with all."

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:23.370
So "all" generally is
a keyword that means

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:26.930
we're doing a broadcast join.

00:20:26.930 --> 00:20:32.680
So it gets tricky when
you need to know--

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:36.140
say we're doing a shuffle
or we're doing a filter.

00:20:36.140 --> 00:20:38.950
It's hard to know how
many shards you're

00:20:38.950 --> 00:20:43.700
going to need in the
subsequent query stages.

00:20:43.700 --> 00:20:45.440
So what we do is we guess.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:47.990
And then if we guess
wrong, we change it.

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:53.440
So here we see in stage two,
we started out with one shard.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:57.344
That shard-- we needed more
data, and added another shard,

00:20:57.344 --> 00:20:59.510
and needed more data, and
we added some more shards.

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:03.704
So this is something that
you'll see very commonly.

00:21:03.704 --> 00:21:05.870
We called it Auto Awesome,
but Auto Awesome actually

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:08.090
has several different flavors.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:09.780
This is just one of them.

00:21:09.780 --> 00:21:13.340
And you can see here,
sometimes in the query plans,

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:15.860
you'll see these
shuffle partitions.

00:21:15.860 --> 00:21:18.320
These are, essentially,
just us realizing that we

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:20.555
have to add more shards.

00:21:24.440 --> 00:21:28.100
So something that people run
into as a problem sometimes

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:30.220
is called a skewed join.

00:21:30.220 --> 00:21:34.730
Now, skewed join is when
one of the keys that you're

00:21:34.730 --> 00:21:39.380
joining against has a
very large amount of data.

00:21:39.380 --> 00:21:41.720
And it generally
only happens when

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:46.970
you have a high cardinality
key that also has--

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:50.280
a single one of those keys
has a large portion of data.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:54.350
So sometimes we see
this in when somebody

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:59.390
is doing a join on, let's
say it's like, product ID.

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:02.250
But half of their product
IDs are the empty string.

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.886
So they might have a million
product IDs and that's

00:22:04.886 --> 00:22:06.260
what they really
want to join on,

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:10.850
but when they do this join
the empty string makes

00:22:10.850 --> 00:22:12.940
it more difficult to schedule.

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:15.140
I'll show why in a second.

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:19.010
So I'm going to simulate this
by running a query where I just

00:22:19.010 --> 00:22:21.810
replaced-- every time
the title has a C in it,

00:22:21.810 --> 00:22:25.790
I replace it with just the
string C. So this will be,

00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:28.357
essentially, half
of the data set.

00:22:28.357 --> 00:22:30.690
And so this is what the query
plan ends up looking like.

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:31.560
So it still runs.

00:22:31.560 --> 00:22:33.530
In general, it still runs.

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:34.620
But it takes a lot longer.

00:22:34.620 --> 00:22:36.770
I think this might
have taken 20 minutes.

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:44.270
And what you can see
here is this compute time

00:22:44.270 --> 00:22:51.080
is all the maximum time.

00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:52.460
This is all the long tail.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:57.440
So we don't even see
the medium shard times.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.297
All we see are the
max shard times.

00:23:00.297 --> 00:23:01.880
And that's one key
to know that you're

00:23:01.880 --> 00:23:06.120
seeing some sort of long
tail or skew effect.

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:08.370
To just show what this looks
like on the architectural

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:14.310
diagram, we have this one
shard is getting a large amount

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:16.572
of data from one side.

00:23:16.572 --> 00:23:18.030
And this shard,
basically, just has

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:20.250
to churn through
all of this data.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:26.594
It has to do a lot more
work than the other shards.

00:23:26.594 --> 00:23:29.010
And we can't just apply the
same sort of re-dispatch trick

00:23:29.010 --> 00:23:32.610
that we did for the
aggregations because we'd need

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.370
to then reshuffle both sides.

00:23:35.370 --> 00:23:38.130
So this is something
that is slow right now.

00:23:38.130 --> 00:23:42.090
It's probable that we
will fix it in the future.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:43.950
But in case you
run into this, it's

00:23:43.950 --> 00:23:47.820
a good thing to know about.

00:23:47.820 --> 00:23:51.960
Another problem that
people hit a lot is they

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:59.220
do a large "order by" and they
hit a resources exceeded error.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:02.530
So what actually happens here?

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:04.710
So let's walk
through an example.

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:08.070
As we were doing
an ORDER BY foo,

00:24:08.070 --> 00:24:10.680
we have a lot of distinct
values or a lot of values

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:13.890
that are read in parallel.

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:17.790
But in order to sort
those, the master node

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:23.790
has to process-- somebody has
to process all of these things

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:26.040
in order to do the sort.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:28.900
And you can overload
the master node.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:32.110
And you can also
run out of memory

00:24:32.110 --> 00:24:35.160
if there are too many things.

00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:40.320
So here's an example of a query
plan that actually hit this.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:44.910
Recently we added support for
returning query plans even when

00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.590
queries fail.

00:24:46.590 --> 00:24:48.850
This can help you diagnose
this kind of problem.

00:24:48.850 --> 00:24:56.130
But here you see, stage two
failed with resource exceeded.

00:24:56.130 --> 00:24:57.460
So you can kind of see.

00:24:57.460 --> 00:24:59.360
And it failed on the sort.

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:03.830
So that's a good indicator
that that's what's going on.

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:05.330
So what do you do
when this happens?

00:25:05.330 --> 00:25:07.620
Well, you can add a limit.

00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:10.440
So in general, if you're
sorting a large amount of data,

00:25:10.440 --> 00:25:18.105
you don't really care about what
happens in row one million to--

00:25:18.105 --> 00:25:20.190
you end up running
that query because that

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:22.230
was the easy thing.

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:24.600
Which usually
people tend to care

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:27.670
about the things at the top
or the things at the bottom.

00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:30.530
So in general, you
can just add a limit.

00:25:30.530 --> 00:25:32.460
And this makes
execution much easier

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:36.820
because we can do the partial
sorts at the lower levels.

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:42.440
And we can drop all of the
ones that exceed the limit.

00:25:42.440 --> 00:25:46.860
And so here we can see
why this is easier.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:51.410
And this is what the query
plan for this looks like.

00:25:51.410 --> 00:25:56.090
One interesting thing to
see is this output number,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:57.590
this 220,000.

00:25:57.590 --> 00:26:04.040
Does anybody have an idea
what this 220,000 represents?

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:07.340
So we saw that there were 220--

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:10.910
220 was the available
parallelism for this table.

00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:12.290
And so the limit is 1,000.

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:18.830
So basically, each one of these
shards is producing 1,000 rows

00:26:18.830 --> 00:26:21.260
and sending those
to the next level.

00:26:24.680 --> 00:26:29.510
And this query
probably ran very fast.

00:26:29.510 --> 00:26:31.760
Just to sort of sum up
some of these resource

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:34.160
exceeded problems
you might run into.

00:26:34.160 --> 00:26:37.640
There are cases
where BigQuery fails

00:26:37.640 --> 00:26:40.050
and it probably should fail.

00:26:40.050 --> 00:26:42.266
So if you're
unintentionally trying

00:26:42.266 --> 00:26:43.640
to generate too
much data-- let's

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:47.940
say you're using string
AGG, which basically will

00:26:47.940 --> 00:26:53.000
concatenate a giant string
with all of the values

00:26:53.000 --> 00:26:55.250
that you're aggregating.

00:26:55.250 --> 00:26:58.130
Now, if you're doing that with
a billion different values,

00:26:58.130 --> 00:27:00.290
you're trying to create
a single string that's

00:27:00.290 --> 00:27:03.080
going to be a terabyte long.

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:05.870
We're going to fail.

00:27:05.870 --> 00:27:08.420
Also, if you're doing
analytic functions, which

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:13.370
are window functions, and you
forget to use a "partition by"

00:27:13.370 --> 00:27:18.230
or you're trying to do this sort
of a massive amount of data,

00:27:18.230 --> 00:27:20.900
that also can cause problems.

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:23.870
Cross join is also a killer.

00:27:23.870 --> 00:27:28.166
If you have 200
two-million row tables

00:27:28.166 --> 00:27:29.540
and you do a cross
join of those,

00:27:29.540 --> 00:27:34.580
you now have a
trillion row table.

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:37.310
But it's easy to
generate massive amounts

00:27:37.310 --> 00:27:41.330
of intermediate data
with a cross join

00:27:41.330 --> 00:27:43.490
and those often will fail.

00:27:43.490 --> 00:27:45.500
So the solution to these,
is to fix your queries

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:48.990
to not do that.

00:27:48.990 --> 00:27:51.500
And then there's also cases
where you probably don't really

00:27:51.500 --> 00:27:54.520
need the results,
which is the ORDER BY.

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:57.510
And to fix that,
you can add a limit.

00:27:57.510 --> 00:27:59.010
And then there's
the cases where you

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:02.385
overload a shard, like the
skewed join that I showed.

00:28:02.385 --> 00:28:04.260
And the solution is to
split up your queries.

00:28:04.260 --> 00:28:07.410
And this is sort of tricky,
but you can basically partition

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:09.070
your query into two pieces.

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:11.610
One is you run the
high cardinality part

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:15.750
and filter out the one
that is overloaded.

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.990
And the second one, you
run just on that one.

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:22.380
And that should be
a much faster query.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:24.120
And then you have to
combine the results.

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:29.780
It's not pleasant, but it works.

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:32.570
So now a little bit
of a digression on how

00:28:32.570 --> 00:28:34.380
to count things in BigQuery.

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.440
So there's really
three different ways

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:43.730
to count things in BigQuery.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:52.040
One is you can use a group
by to come up with the count.

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:56.390
This is kind of a pain
to write in queries.

00:28:56.390 --> 00:28:59.174
Also, if you were trying to
count multiple distinct things

00:28:59.174 --> 00:29:00.590
at the same time,
it can be really

00:29:00.590 --> 00:29:03.200
difficult to write the queries.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:08.550
But it's an exact and reasonably
scalable way to do it.

00:29:08.550 --> 00:29:10.460
The other way is count distinct.

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:14.660
One of the things that people
hated about the BigQuery Legacy

00:29:14.660 --> 00:29:20.210
SQL was that count distinct
was approximate, by default.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:24.320
And we have since fixed that
in BigQuery Standard SQL.

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:27.260
We have quite a nice
implementation of count

00:29:27.260 --> 00:29:28.269
distinct.

00:29:28.269 --> 00:29:29.810
But it is going to
be reasonably slow

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:32.270
because I'll show in a
second why accounting

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:34.670
distinct values is hard.

00:29:34.670 --> 00:29:37.430
And third we have
approximate count distinct,

00:29:37.430 --> 00:29:42.194
which is very fast, but
it is an approximation.

00:29:42.194 --> 00:29:44.360
This is sort of a new
implementation that we've just

00:29:44.360 --> 00:29:50.438
added that uses an algorithm
called HyperLogLog++.

00:29:50.438 --> 00:29:54.650
So HyperLogLog++
uses HyperLogLog,

00:29:54.650 --> 00:30:00.570
which is an algorithm for
doing cardinality estimation.

00:30:00.570 --> 00:30:06.230
And HyperLogLog++ is just
Google's version of that that

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:12.674
applies using a lot of
experience in using this and it

00:30:12.674 --> 00:30:13.340
tweaks it a bit.

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:19.700
It has better estimations
at certain cardinality

00:30:19.700 --> 00:30:23.090
ranges, better accuracy,
and uses less memory,

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.232
and is faster.

00:30:25.232 --> 00:30:27.440
In general, you don't have
to know that that's what's

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:29.540
being used under the covers.

00:30:29.540 --> 00:30:32.990
You just get accurate
count distinct answers.

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:38.780
So these are some benchmarks
that I ran against our data set

00:30:38.780 --> 00:30:40.350
of various sizes.

00:30:40.350 --> 00:30:47.780
And we can see that error
is staying under 1%.

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:51.720
But the performance
is much, much faster.

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:54.770
And these are log
lines so it really

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:59.060
is much, much faster
on large data sets.

00:30:59.060 --> 00:31:01.790
So we've also added some
HyperLogLog functions

00:31:01.790 --> 00:31:05.880
that give you a little
more control over this.

00:31:05.880 --> 00:31:11.300
This is kind of geeky, but I
find it pretty interesting.

00:31:11.300 --> 00:31:14.120
So one of the things
that you might want to do

00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:17.180
is you might want to count
distinct users by language

00:31:17.180 --> 00:31:21.440
from the get help data
set, where language

00:31:21.440 --> 00:31:23.630
is the programming language.

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:28.550
So this takes, it
looks like, 23 minutes

00:31:28.550 --> 00:31:31.850
to run because you're
running count distinct.

00:31:31.850 --> 00:31:34.790
But really you probably don't
care about the exact values.

00:31:34.790 --> 00:31:37.020
So you can run--

00:31:37.020 --> 00:31:40.340
if you use approx
count distinct,

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:42.570
it'll run like 60 times faster.

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:46.580
So that's 22 seconds instead.

00:31:46.580 --> 00:31:49.310
But one of the issues when
you're counting distinct

00:31:49.310 --> 00:31:50.540
values--

00:31:50.540 --> 00:31:54.680
and this holds whether
you're using an approx count

00:31:54.680 --> 00:31:56.570
or an exact count.

00:31:56.570 --> 00:31:58.790
But if you try to
take some approximate

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:01.190
counts and then sum those up--

00:32:01.190 --> 00:32:04.400
so if we wanted to get the sum
of distinct users per language

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:08.150
and then find the total number
of distinct users overall,

00:32:08.150 --> 00:32:10.250
those numbers can't be summed.

00:32:10.250 --> 00:32:13.340
Because some of those
same users that were using

00:32:13.340 --> 00:32:15.770
Perl are also using Ruby.

00:32:15.770 --> 00:32:18.270
And it's hard to
know what those are.

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:25.150
So there are some
HyperLogLog functions

00:32:25.150 --> 00:32:29.830
that lets you split up the
process of this counting

00:32:29.830 --> 00:32:33.030
into different pieces.

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:38.570
And the first one is,
it builds a sketch.

00:32:38.570 --> 00:32:45.600
A sketch is a probabilistic
mapping of the values

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:48.270
and the cardinality
of the values.

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:50.620
And so, you can break
it up into two steps.

00:32:50.620 --> 00:32:52.751
First you do the in it,
which creates the sketch.

00:32:52.751 --> 00:32:54.750
The second, you do the
extract and this actually

00:32:54.750 --> 00:33:09.000
will return the same value that
the other approx count distinct

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:10.146
returned.

00:33:10.146 --> 00:33:11.520
So why would you
want to do this?

00:33:11.520 --> 00:33:16.900
Well, one option is,
sometimes you have PII

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:18.409
and you want to do aggregations.

00:33:18.409 --> 00:33:19.950
But sometimes if
you do aggregations,

00:33:19.950 --> 00:33:24.660
then you lose the distinctness.

00:33:24.660 --> 00:33:27.730
You don't know how many
unique users you have.

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:31.230
So this lets you save a
relatively small sketch.

00:33:31.230 --> 00:33:33.390
And then, finally, you
can do the extract, which

00:33:33.390 --> 00:33:35.555
computes the distinct values.

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:42.880
But the kind of cool
thing about these sketches

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:45.800
is you can combine the sketches.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:47.470
So if you do merge,
you can actually

00:33:47.470 --> 00:33:57.310
compute the sum of
each distinct sketch.

00:33:57.310 --> 00:34:06.930
So you can come up with the
total sum of all distinct users

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:08.010
in get hub.

00:34:08.010 --> 00:34:11.070
And so the overall
number you get

00:34:11.070 --> 00:34:12.659
is the exact same
number as if you'd

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:17.010
done the number of
approx count distinct.

00:34:17.010 --> 00:34:20.370
So to summarize
these, you can use

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:23.969
these to anonymize user IDs.

00:34:23.969 --> 00:34:24.795
You can use these--

00:34:27.870 --> 00:34:31.190
so there's multiple
HLL functions.

00:34:31.190 --> 00:34:36.050
We should be releasing
documentation on those shortly.

00:34:36.050 --> 00:34:40.120
OK, so I'm going
to go another demo.

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:45.610
And so now that I've shown
how to do this in general,

00:34:45.610 --> 00:34:48.889
let's walk through
a query that is slow

00:34:48.889 --> 00:34:51.199
and show how it would actually
optimize it in practice.

00:34:55.591 --> 00:34:57.543
If I can type my password in.

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:10.770
OK, so for this one,
I'm going to use

00:35:10.770 --> 00:35:12.540
one of my favorite tables here.

00:35:12.540 --> 00:35:20.580
And this a petabyte data set
that we built for GCP Next

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:21.840
last year.

00:35:21.840 --> 00:35:25.290
So this table is 1.1 petabytes.

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:28.530
It's a trillion rows.

00:35:28.530 --> 00:35:30.310
It's part of this
retail data set

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:33.690
that looks like a
retail schema that you

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:36.460
might be used to seeing.

00:35:36.460 --> 00:35:40.890
We applied some kind of
advance BigQuery techniques

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:43.320
to this that are best practices.

00:35:43.320 --> 00:35:46.200
So here, instead of
having a sales table

00:35:46.200 --> 00:35:50.130
and a line-item
table, we basically

00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:53.190
denormalized that
into a single row

00:35:53.190 --> 00:35:57.639
that has the sale and the
line item as a nested value.

00:35:57.639 --> 00:35:59.180
So you can see how
we have this here.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:00.690
We have the sale
and then we have

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:05.370
each of the line
items within that row.

00:36:05.370 --> 00:36:11.280
But there's still a
trillion outer entries.

00:36:11.280 --> 00:36:14.220
So actually the inner
entries are probably

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:16.840
something on the order
of 10 to 20 trillion.

00:36:22.030 --> 00:36:25.460
OK, so here's the query
that I want to run.

00:36:25.460 --> 00:36:27.950
Hopefully that's a
little bit legible.

00:36:27.950 --> 00:36:31.670
But I want to show
that, yeah, you

00:36:31.670 --> 00:36:38.240
can do complex queries
over data of this size.

00:36:38.240 --> 00:36:41.580
So one of the techniques
we're doing here

00:36:41.580 --> 00:36:45.440
is we're looking at--

00:36:45.440 --> 00:36:47.390
since this table is
partitioned, we're

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:48.980
going to do a partition filter.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:53.210
So we're going to just
do one month of data.

00:36:53.210 --> 00:36:55.650
We're also using
a window function.

00:36:55.650 --> 00:36:57.950
So we're doing a
partition in order

00:36:57.950 --> 00:37:00.587
by to compute the
running average of sales

00:37:00.587 --> 00:37:01.295
for each product.

00:37:04.310 --> 00:37:12.070
And we are also doing a join.

00:37:12.070 --> 00:37:17.687
So we're joining from this giant
table to our products table.

00:37:17.687 --> 00:37:19.270
And I don't want to
make you guys wait

00:37:19.270 --> 00:37:24.430
while I ran this query,
but I ran it right before.

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:27.250
And it took two minutes.

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:30.780
Now one distinct thing here
is it only processed 1.8--

00:37:30.780 --> 00:37:35.244
or only processed two terabytes.

00:37:35.244 --> 00:37:36.910
So there's a couple
of reasons for that,

00:37:36.910 --> 00:37:38.326
even though it's
a petabyte table.

00:37:38.326 --> 00:37:42.100
One is we only bill you for
the columns that you read.

00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:44.920
So we only read a
few of the columns.

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:47.387
And second is we only bill
you for the partitions

00:37:47.387 --> 00:37:47.970
that you read.

00:37:47.970 --> 00:37:51.797
So we only needed to
read the one month's

00:37:51.797 --> 00:37:52.630
worth of partitions.

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:54.970
But there were still
a lot of data here.

00:37:54.970 --> 00:38:00.290
And we did run a regular
expression over a lot of data.

00:38:00.290 --> 00:38:02.710
So here's what the
query plan looks like.

00:38:07.170 --> 00:38:09.360
So what do we see
from this query plan?

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:11.600
So we see that stage
one is actually

00:38:11.600 --> 00:38:15.590
where we spent almost
all of our time.

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:20.600
And we notice that the
output rows were larger

00:38:20.600 --> 00:38:21.530
than the input rows.

00:38:21.530 --> 00:38:23.240
And so that seems
a little bit funny.

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:29.630
But one of the things that we
had to do here in this query

00:38:29.630 --> 00:38:35.330
is we ended up flattening
these line items.

00:38:35.330 --> 00:38:40.280
So each of the line
items had to be unnested.

00:38:44.250 --> 00:38:46.610
And so this is where
we spent a lot of time.

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:50.337
Now these look like
repartitioned stages.

00:38:50.337 --> 00:38:52.670
And if you look at that then,
yes, that's what they are.

00:38:55.730 --> 00:38:59.630
So it looks like this stage
processed 10 billion rows.

00:38:59.630 --> 00:39:01.040
That's an interesting one.

00:39:01.040 --> 00:39:02.300
So we look in here.

00:39:02.300 --> 00:39:05.030
And we can see that we
didn't "each all" join.

00:39:05.030 --> 00:39:08.000
So we did a broadcast join.

00:39:08.000 --> 00:39:11.480
So that's positive news.

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:14.140
We know that we didn't
have to shuffle.

00:39:14.140 --> 00:39:16.250
We didn't have the
shuffle actually these

00:39:16.250 --> 00:39:21.065
10 billion rows because that
would have been expensive.

00:39:24.320 --> 00:39:24.980
Or did we?

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:27.440
Actually we did.

00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:31.237
So actually, that was expensive.

00:39:40.640 --> 00:39:43.110
So this is the stage where
we read the products table.

00:39:43.110 --> 00:39:46.730
And so the products table
had two million products

00:39:46.730 --> 00:39:51.050
and we filtered it down
to 600 products, which is

00:39:51.050 --> 00:39:53.930
why we could do the broadcast.

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:57.420
But actually, it only started
out as two million products.

00:39:57.420 --> 00:40:00.530
So one of the things we
could do is, so we're

00:40:00.530 --> 00:40:02.420
applying this filter here.

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:05.070
We're filtering on the--

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:07.375
and this is actually one
nice thing in Standard SQL

00:40:07.375 --> 00:40:08.750
is we actually
have an optimizer.

00:40:08.750 --> 00:40:16.400
We can push this
filter inside the join.

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:20.360
But we're filtering on the
key wallets in products.

00:40:20.360 --> 00:40:26.930
If we change this to filter the
key wallets in the sales table,

00:40:26.930 --> 00:40:27.860
we'll end up having--

00:40:27.860 --> 00:40:31.970
we can push this all the
way into the storage layer.

00:40:31.970 --> 00:40:36.020
We can end up having to
read out a lot less data.

00:40:36.020 --> 00:40:40.280
And hopefully, this query
should go should go faster.

00:40:40.280 --> 00:40:42.320
So let's see how
long this takes.

00:40:52.210 --> 00:40:56.410
Come on BigQuery.

00:40:56.410 --> 00:40:58.800
So 30 seconds.

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:04.370
So that's a lot
faster, factor of six

00:41:04.370 --> 00:41:05.940
to give us the same answer.

00:41:05.940 --> 00:41:08.780
Now notice one other option
we could have had was to--

00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:13.430
we could have run it on a
smaller set of partitions.

00:41:13.430 --> 00:41:16.230
But we want to do the
trailing weekly average

00:41:16.230 --> 00:41:18.200
and so if you do it a
lot less than a month,

00:41:18.200 --> 00:41:19.245
then that's going to--

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:25.650
you may not get the type
of answers that you want.

00:41:25.650 --> 00:41:28.490
So here we see that
the big difference

00:41:28.490 --> 00:41:30.950
is in this first
stage where we still

00:41:30.950 --> 00:41:34.070
had to read the underlying
seven billion rows,

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:37.550
but we all had to
output 4 billion.

00:41:37.550 --> 00:41:41.510
And we can see that this
filter, this regular expression,

00:41:41.510 --> 00:41:45.760
was pushed down into
this layer here.

00:41:51.730 --> 00:41:54.240
And can we switch
back the presentation?

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:03.120
OK, so that's all I have.

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:05.700
If you like BigQuery, there
are some other presentations

00:42:05.700 --> 00:42:07.212
you might be interested in.

00:42:07.212 --> 00:42:08.670
There was a data
model-- obviously,

00:42:08.670 --> 00:42:10.560
these have already
passed, but they

00:42:10.560 --> 00:42:16.510
will be online so that you can
stream them at your leisure.

00:42:16.510 --> 00:42:18.410
But thank you.

